        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>From the Heights to Clear Lake: where glass railings go in Houston</strong></p><p>Houston has no zoning, so a 1920s bungalow in the Heights can sit next to a three-story townhome with a rooftop deck. Those inner-loop townhomes, in Rice Military, Montrose, Midtown and EaDo, are the natural fit for a glass deck railing on a roof. Memorial, River Oaks and West University lean toward larger lots with a pool, a covered patio and sometimes a second-floor balcony over the backyard. The master-planned suburbs follow the same pattern at scale. The Woodlands is to the north, Katy and Cinco Ranch to the west, Sugar Land to the southwest and Pearland to the south. Down on Galveston Bay, Clear Lake, Seabrook, Kemah and League City have raised waterfront homes with balconies facing the water. That is where salt air enters the picture. Wherever the address, the crate, the hardware and the delivery are the same.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Houston building code, permits and the no-zoning question</strong></p><p>No zoning does not mean no code. The City of Houston applies the 2021 International Residential Code and International Building Code with local amendments. Permits go through the Houston Permitting Center. The code sets a 36\" minimum guard height for a house deck, balcony or rooftop deck and 42\" for commercial and multifamily buildings. For pool barriers, most cities in the Houston area apply the 48\" rule from the IRC and ISPSC. Texas Health and Safety Code chapter 757 sets 48\" for apartment and HOA pools too. West University Place, Bellaire, the Memorial villages, Sugar Land and Pearland each have their own building department. Check with the one that covers your lot. The Woodlands adds its own design review under the community covenants. The summary for the whole state is on our <a href=\"/pages/texas-building-code-for-glass-railings\">Texas building code page</a>, and the <a href=\"/pages/glass-railing-texas\">Texas hub</a> links every city we cover.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Humidity, hurricanes and freight delivery in Houston</strong></p><p>Houston weather is wet, hot and occasionally violent. The hardware answer is simple: every spigot, connector, hinge and latch is 2205 marine-grade stainless steel with a 10-year no-rust promise. That is why it suits Clear Lake and Kemah as well as Katy. Hurricane season runs June through November, and a building department may ask how exterior elements are attached. Our <a href=\"/pages/glass-railing-engineering-testing\">engineering and testing documents</a> for top-mount and side-mount spigots on wood and concrete are there for that review. Delivery is by freight carrier with a tailgate lift. Orders are processed in 2 to 3 business days and delivered in 5 to 10 business days. On a narrow Heights or Montrose street, the driver needs room to set the crate at the curb or driveway. Clear a spot before the truck calls. Check the glass within 3 days of delivery and before installation.</p>"
          }
        }
